            'body_en' => "Amedi stands on a mesa in the valley of the Great Zab, in the far north of the Kurdistan Region, about 1,200 metres above the sea and the most northerly town in Iraq — mild in summer, and snowed on in winter. The flat top is a little over a kilometre long, and the town has always filled it exactly: the cliff is the city wall.\n\nThe chronicler Ibn al-Athir says the name comes from Imad al-Din Zengi, the atabeg of Mosul, who built a citadel here in 1142 on the site of an older fort called Āšib; others hear something Hurrian or Urartian in it, far older. The valley below had belonged to Subartu, to Mitanni, to Assyria and to the Medes before the Parthians cut their figures on the rock, and Xenophon marched past in 401 BC through what he called ruined Median cities.\n\nFrom the fourteenth century until 1843 Amedi was the capital of Bahdinan, one of the most durable of the Kurdish emirates. Its emirs claimed descent from the Abbasid caliphs — Yazidi tradition remembers them instead as the line of a Yazidi saint, Amadin — and they ruled a territory that ran from Akre to Zakho, reaching its height under Bahram Pasha between 1726 and 1767. The end came from a neighbour, not an empire: in 1831–33 Mir Muhammad of Soran took Akre, Amedi and Zakho, and the weakened emirate was folded into the Ottoman province of Mosul within a decade. It was a town of three peoples: in 1907 its six thousand inhabitants were 2,500 Kurds, 1,900 Jews and 1,600 Chaldean Catholic Assyrians.",
